=== layout: subsection

# 19. Consumer-Oriented or Trivial Transgenics

=== layout: bookend

# 19. Questions

@body

- What are examples of genetically modified animals created mainly for appearance, decoration, or entertainment, such as GloFish?
- How do regulatory agencies decide whether a "trivial" transgenic organism is safe to sell or display?
- What artistic projects have used transgenic animals, and how were these projects received by the public?
- Do designer organisms blur the boundary between art and biotechnology, and should there be limits on how far this can go?
- Should genetic modification be restricted to health, agriculture, and research, or can nonessential uses be acceptable in society?
- How might future consumer trends push companies to design even more unusual transgenic pets?

=== layout: one-panel

# 19. GloFish and a Visible Trait

@body

- Fluorescent proteins absorb light and emit light at a different wavelength.
- GloFish use an engineered fluorescent trait for appearance.
- The scientific mechanism can be similar to a lab marker even when the product has a different purpose.

=== layout: one-panel

# 19. Would the Purpose Change Your Decision?

@body

- Compare a fluorescent pet with a fluorescent laboratory animal.
- For each one, name the benefit, who receives it, and the welfare or environmental concern.
- Would your decision change if the fish could persist after escape?

=== layout: one-panel

# 19. Alba and the Purpose of Bioart

@body

- Eduardo Kac presented a fluorescent rabbit named Alba as part of a work of art.
- Compare the artist's purpose with the purpose of a fluorescent marker in research.
- What responsibilities does an artist have when the work involves a living animal?

@citation

[Eduardo Kac, GFP Bunny project](https://ekac.org/gfpbunny_essay.html)

=== layout: big-image

@image

![Advertising a Fluorescent Trait](assets/set7_instructor/glofish_ad.jpg)

@caption

Topic 19: Advertising a Fluorescent Trait\
